The '''Tozai Line''' (東西線 ''Tōzai-sen'') is Line 2 of the [[Okami Metro]], the rapid transit system serving the city of [[Okami]] in [[Okaiken]]. Coloured orange (#f4a460) on system maps, the line runs approximately 64.2 kilometres from [[Takanoko]] in the west to [[Hojo Daigaku-mae]] in the east, serving 64 stations along its route. The Tozai Line is the longest line in the Okami Metro network by station count, and serves as the primary east–west cross-city corridor, connecting the western suburbs of Takanoko and Kume through the central business districts of Aoi and Chiyoda to the eastern industrial and residential zones of Higashi, Minami, and Tobe.

Opened in 1961 as the second subway line in Okami, the Tozai Line was built to relieve severe surface congestion on the east–west tram corridor that had served as the city's primary cross-town surface route since the early 20th century. The line's construction marked a significant expansion of the city's underground transit network, complementing the existing [[Miyoshi Line]] and establishing the grid pattern that characterizes the Okami Metro to this day. With 20 interchange stations, the Tozai Line intersects nearly every other line in the network and plays a critical role in facilitating cross-city journeys.

The Tozai Line is notable for its [[through-service]] operations with [[Okaiken Railways]] commuter lines, allowing trains to continue beyond the network's boundaries onto surface railway tracks. This integration provides seamless travel from the western suburbs through the city centre to the eastern industrial zones without requiring passengers to transfer. The line operates using 6-car Type C trains at a maximum speed of 100 km/h, powered by 1,500 V DC overhead catenary on standard 1,435 mm gauge track.

== History ==

The Tozai Line's origins lie in the growing congestion of Okami's east–west surface tram corridor during the post-war economic boom. By the late 1950s, the tram line that ran along the central east–west corridor was operating at well over capacity, with frequent delays and overcrowding during peak hours. The Okami Municipal Transit Bureau proposed an underground railway to replace the busiest section of the tram route, and construction was approved in 1958. The initial segment opened in 1961, making the Tozai Line the second subway line in Okami after the [[Miyoshi Line]].

The line was extended gradually through the 1960s and 1970s, reaching westward into the growing suburban districts of Oka, Kume, and Takanoko. The eastern extensions proved more challenging, as they required tunnelling beneath the densely developed Tama and Tamade wards. The Tama ward section is notable for its tight station spacing, a result of the extremely dense urban development in the area and the need to serve as many residential blocks as possible. Some stations in this section are less than 800 metres apart, among the shortest inter-station distances in the Okami Metro network.

Extensions continued through the 1980s and 1990s, with the line reaching the eastern wards of Higashi and Minami in stages. The final extension to [[Hojo Daigaku-mae]] in the Tobe ward was completed in the early 2000s, bringing the line to its current length of 64.2 km and 64 stations. In recent years, several station mergers have been implemented in the Tama section to improve operational efficiency, as the extremely close station spacing was causing significant delays and reducing overall line capacity. These mergers have been controversial among local residents but have generally improved travel times.

The through-service arrangement with [[Okaiken Railways]] was established in the 1990s and has since been expanded. The integration allows Tozai Line trains to continue beyond [[Takanoko]] onto Okaiken Railways western commuter lines, and beyond [[Hojo Daigaku-mae]] onto eastern commuter services. This through-service arrangement has been widely praised for reducing transfer burdens and is considered a model for future integration projects in Okaiken. The line was privatized in 2018 along with the rest of the Okami Metro network.

== Route description ==

The Tozai Line begins at [[Takanoko]] in the far western suburbs of Okami, running eastward through the residential districts of Takanoko and Kume. The western section features relatively wide station spacing, reflecting the lower-density suburban character of these areas. As the line enters the Oka ward, the station spacing narrows considerably, and the line begins to serve denser commercial and residential districts.

The central section of the line passes through the Uonuma, Nishi, and Aoi wards, where it intersects with the [[Miyoshi Line]] at several key stations including Kamiya-cho and Hozumi-Oiwake. This central section is the busiest part of the line, carrying commuters through the heart of the city's business and commercial districts. The line continues eastward through the Nagata and Chiyoda wards, crossing the city's central north–south axis.

In the eastern section, the line passes through the Tama ward, where the tight station spacing is most pronounced. Despite recent station mergers, this section still has some of the closest inter-station distances in the network. The line then continues through Tamade, Rokumanji, and Minato wards before reaching the Higashi and Minami wards. The easternmost section runs through the Tobe ward, serving the industrial and educational districts before terminating at [[Hojo Daigaku-mae]], which serves the nearby Hojo University campus.

