== Rolling stock ==

=== Current ===
* '''6000 series''' — The primary rolling stock of the Tozai Line, introduced in 2020 by [[Okami Heavy Industries]]. These 6-car Type C trains were designed as the successor to the 2000 series and feature modern passenger information systems, energy-efficient propulsion, and improved accessibility. The 6000 series is shared with other Type C lines in the Okami Metro network and is equipped for through-service operations with Okaiken Railways, featuring compatible signalling and control systems.
* '''2000 series''' — A legacy fleet dating to 1981, these trains were extensively refurbished in 2018 to extend their service life. The refurbishment included updated interiors, new propulsion systems, and improved lighting. The 2000 series is being progressively phased out as additional 6000 series trains are delivered. The remaining 2000 series trains primarily operate during off-peak hours and on shorter through-service runs.

=== Historical ===
* '''3000 series''' — Introduced in the 1970s for the eastern extensions. Retired in the early 2000s.
* '''1000 series (Tozai)''' — The original rolling stock used when the line opened in 1961. Retired in the 1980s.

== Through-service ==

The Tozai Line operates through-service with [[Okaiken Railways]] commuter lines at both ends of the route. At the western terminus of [[Takanoko]], trains continue onto Okaiken Railways western commuter lines, serving the outer suburban communities beyond the Okami Metro network. At the eastern terminus of [[Hojo Daigaku-mae]], trains also connect via the Toukai–Fukukyu Connecting Link to the [[Fukukyu Fukushima Line]] and eastern commuter services, linking the industrial zones and university campuses of the eastern corridor.

The through-service arrangement was first established in the 1990s and has been progressively expanded since. To enable through-service operations, the 6000 series rolling stock was designed with compatible signalling, control, and power systems that meet Okaiken Railways standards. This integration allows for seamless travel from the western suburbs through the city centre to the eastern industrial zones without requiring passengers to transfer, significantly reducing journey times for cross-regional commuters.

The through-service is one of the most heavily used features of the Okami Metro system, with approximately 30% of Tozai Line trains continuing beyond the network boundaries during peak hours. The arrangement has been cited as a model for future transit integration projects across Okaiken and has influenced similar developments in other metropolitan areas.

